@brief
The bare line in this runbook should be a markdown link to the refunds
doc:

    [Refund window policy](docs/refunds.md)

So the line needs something at the front and something at the back, and
you already know two keys that do exactly that. This lesson is about a
third way of getting between them: borrowing a single normal-mode command
without leaving insert mode at all.

@keys
| `I` | insert at the first non-blank character |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `Ctrl-O` | run one normal-mode command, then return to insert |
| `$` | end of the line |
| `Esc` | out |

@hints
You can do this with `I`, `Esc`, `A`. Do it that way first so it is
finished, then come back and try it in one insert session.
--
`Ctrl-O` suspends insert mode for exactly one command. `Ctrl-O $` moves
you to the end of the line and leaves you still typing.
--
`jj` to the line, `I`, type `[`, then `Ctrl-O` `$`, then type the rest,
`Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    j j I [ <Ctrl-O> $ ](docs/refunds.md) <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Twenty-nine keystrokes. The `I` … `Esc` `A` … route is twenty-nine too.
They tie, and that is the honest answer: `Ctrl-O` is not a keystroke
saving.

What it buys you is that the whole thing stays one insert. One undo step,
so `u` takes the link back in one press instead of two. And when what you
need mid-typing is a command with no insert-mode equivalent at all, it is
the only way to have it without stopping:

| `Ctrl-O` `$` | to the end of the line |
| `Ctrl-O` `0` | to the front of it |
| `Ctrl-O` `zz` | redraw with the cursor centred, when you have typed to the bottom of the screen |
| `Ctrl-O` `D` | delete from the cursor to the end of the line — chapter 8 |

Now the warning, because this key is a trap for people at your stage.
`Ctrl-O` makes it possible to never leave insert mode, and last lesson was
about why that is a bad deal. Treat it as a key for *one* command in the
middle of a genuine burst of typing. If you find yourself pressing
`Ctrl-O` twice in a row, you did not want `Ctrl-O`. You wanted `Esc`.

